{"@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[
{"@type":"Question","name":"How long does Toronto take to review a building permit for a house?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The Ontario Building Code gives the City 10 business days to review a complete application for a house. If the examiner sends comments, the revised drawings are reviewed again."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Why is my permit taking longer than 10 business days?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The timeframe only starts once the application is complete, and it covers one review. Missing documents, rounds of comments, zoning issues and extra approvals all add time."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"How long does a minor variance take in Toronto?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It usually adds a few months: the application is circulated, neighbours are notified, the Committee of Adjustment decides at a hearing, and there is a 20-day appeal period after the decision. An appeal adds more time."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I start work before the permit is issued?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Work that needs a permit should not start until the permit is issued, and inspections follow at set stages during construction."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"How can I speed up my permit?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Check zoning before the design is final, include the structural engineering in the first submission, submit a complete package, and answer the examiner's comments quickly and completely."}}]}
